**********Source Check**********

The claim 'International travelers can now use `something` on China’s biggest payment apps.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The text states, "Alipay and WeChat Pay started to comprehensively upgrade their systems in July 2023 to optimize support for international bank cards to better serve a growing number of international travelers." It further elaborates that "If Jim was a first-time user of WeChat Pay from abroad, he could also directly add his international bank cards to quickly activate the WeChat payment function" and "If he uses Alipay with real-name registration, he would be able to make a single transaction with a maximum limit of $5,000 and a maximum annual transaction limit of $50,000." This explicitly confirms that international travelers can now use their international bank cards on China's biggest payment apps (Alipay and WeChat Pay). </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim '`At a certain time`, Alipay and WeChat started allowing the use of foreign credit cards on their apps.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The article explicitly states that "Alipay and WeChat Pay started to comprehensively upgrade their systems in July 2023 to optimize support for international bank cards." This indicates the time when they began allowing the use of foreign credit cards. </accept>

**********Source Check**********

The claim 'Alipay and WeChat Pay have `in a certain way` to accept foreign credit cards.' is supported by the citation.
<accept> The article explicitly states that "Alipay and WeChat Pay started to comprehensively upgrade their systems in July 2023 to optimize support for international bank cards," and further explains that foreigners can "directly add international bank cards to quickly activate the WeChat payment function" and use Alipay "with real-name registration" to link international cards, specifying transaction limits. This describes the "way" they accept foreign credit cards. </accept>

**********Exact Claim Check**********

The claim 'International travelers can now use Visa and Mastercard on China’s biggest payment apps.' is supported by the original webpage.
<accept> The text explicitly states that international travelers can now link cards issued by Visa and Mastercard to China’s biggest payment apps, Alipay and WeChat Pay, and use them for payments across the country. For example, it says, "Alipay... announced on Friday that overseas users can link cards issued by Visa and Mastercard... to their digital wallets. The change takes effect immediately." and "WeChat users can now link credit or debit cards issued by Visa, Mastercard... for payment at tens of millions of merchants in China." </accept>

**********Exact Claim Check**********

The claim 'In 2019, Alipay and WeChat started allowing the use of foreign credit cards on their apps.' is supported by the original webpage.
<accept> The content explicitly states: "In 2019, Alipay and WeChat started allowing the use of foreign credit cards on their apps as Chinese regulators eased restrictions." </accept>

**********Exact Claim Check**********

The claim 'Alipay and WeChat Pay have revived efforts to accept foreign credit cards.' is supported by the original webpage.
<accept> The article explicitly states, "The move marks a revival of efforts by Alipay and WeChat Pay to accept foreign credit cards after they provided some limited access in late 2019." </accept>

